  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><b>Set up a single AWS OpsWorks stack with two layers, one for Java/Tomcat and the other for Node.js, then design a single custom recipe for provisioning.</b> is correct because it leverages OpsWorks stacks & layers to logically organize your application's architecture: the Java/Tomcat app and the new Node.js chat module can reside in different layers within the same stack, allowing full management and automation tailored to each application stack (e.g., memory-optimized instances for Java and potentially compute-optimized for Node.js). Maintaining a single stack reduces administrative overhead, and a single custom recipe streamlines deployment workflows and feature additions while minimizing duplication.</p><p>The option <i>Build two AWS OpsWorks stacks (one per application) each with a dedicated layer, each managed by its own custom recipe.</i> adds unnecessary complexity and management burden, fragmenting the deployment of related services that should be tightly coordinated. <i>Create a single AWS OpsWorks stack, a shared layer for both applications, and manage with one custom recipe.</i> fails to isolate the different runtime and resource requirements of Java and Node.js, which are best administered via separate layers. <i>Design two AWS OpsWorks stacks. Use two layers and only one custom recipe for both.</i> also unnecessarily splits the infra while duplicating effort when a single stack would suffice.</p><p><b>Tips:</b> When integrating multi-tier or multi-platform applications, separate layers for each runtime or component within the same OpsWorks stack provide both logical separation and centralized stack management, improving maintainability and consistency.</p>",
  "query": "<p>An innovative web startup hosts a thriving social news platform using Amazon EC2 for compute (behind an Elastic Load Balancer and managed by an Auto Scaling group of Java/Tomcat app servers), with DynamoDB as its data backend. The main website is highly memory-dependent, performing best on m2.xlarge instances. Each new code release demands the periodic (about once a week) semi-automated creation, validation, and baking of new application server AMIs—a process that is fairly time-consuming. Now, a Node.js-powered chat component has been developed and needs to be seamlessly integrated into the overall solution. As a solutions architect, which AWS OpsWorks strategy will support deploying and managing both the main Java/Tomcat app and the new chat service most effectively?</p>",
